description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/substrate-gap.ts, stop the falsifier classifier from destroying a gap writer's closure predicate.

Replace this line, which occurs exactly once in the file:
    merged["falsifier"] = c.falsifier;

with exactly these two lines:
    if (typeof merged["falsifier"] === "string" && (merged["falsifier"] as string).length > 24) merged["falsifier_predicate"] = merged["falsifier"];
    merged["falsifier"] = c.falsifier;

WHY: the field classification_metadata.falsifier is overloaded. Gap filers write a free-text closure PREDICATE into it, while classifyFalsifier returns a short class LABEL — one of none, class1, class2 or unresolvable. The assignment overwrites the predicate with the label, so no gap in the store can retain a closure predicate and gaps can only be closed by expiry rather than on evidence. This was proven with a sentinel: a 120-character predicate written into a new gap was absent from the stored row seconds later, while another key from the same metadata object survived. The comment directly above this line already promises ADD BESIDE, NEVER REWRITE and states that the writer predicate survives byte-identical; today it does not.

WHY THIS SHAPE OF FIX: the label must stay in falsifier because falsifierCoverage, the only other reader, counts that field against the keys class1, class2, unresolvable and none. Renaming the field would make every gap count as unstamped. Copying a substantive predicate aside first is additive, changes no existing reader, and restores the behaviour the comment describes. The length test of greater than 24 characters distinguishes a real predicate from the short class labels, the longest of which is unresolvable at 12 characters.

Do not change any other line in the file. Do not reformat surrounding code.

POST-CONDITION: after this change the file contains the exact string falsifier_predicate, the line assigning c.falsifier to merged falsifier is still present immediately after the new guard line, and the file typechecks clean.

